{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And all his sons arose, etc.</b> No words of consolation are mentioned here." "<b>And all his sons arose, etc.</b> No words of consolation are mentioned here. And it appears that the verse's intention is: when his sons saw such intense mourning and sackcloth on his loins for many days, they said: 'This is what a man does when his only son dies, or even if he is not an only son, if he had few sons he would be particular about the loss of even one of the few.' Therefore they devised a plan by which he would be comforted on his own, namely that all his sons gathered together — eleven sons and eleven daughters, and their sons and their daughters, a great multitude — and they came and stood beside him, and this would be his consolation. For whoever has so many sons and daughters, it is not fitting for him to grieve so greatly over a younger son if he is missing from the sons. And this is what the verse says: <b>and all his sons and all his daughters arose</b> together, and this standing of all of them together was to comfort him when he would see how numerous his sons and daughters were. Yet even so, this availed him nothing and he refused to be comforted, and he stated the reason: <b>כִּי אֵרֵד אֶל בְּנִי</b> — meaning, in the sense that I have only him uniquely, for all his sons and daughters are not sufficient to be consoled for him, because he loved him more than all his brothers, for the reason known in the words of the Zohar (part 1, 180a)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in its saying <b>and he refused</b> etc. <b>for I will go down</b> etc., meaning: even though he saw that he refused to be comforted, one should not learn from this that a living person adds [mourning] and does not accept consolation for the living, for he stated the reason for the absence of consolation on account of the reason 'for I will go down to my son.' And our Sages of blessed memory said that a sign was placed in his hand, etc., up to here." "<b>And his father wept for him.</b> Meaning, when he had said his words he returned to weep for him. And the verse was precise in saying <b>his father</b>, to exclude all his sons and all [his daughters] etc., who did not weep at the mention [of him], but only his father. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 84:21) that this is Isaac, the father of Jacob — and this is a midrash."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:58.221837877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}